  "query_preview": "..Moisture is starting to push into the southwestern\nportions of Utah with reflectivity on radar, however this rain is\nstruggling to make it to the surface. RH across these areas are \nbetween 20% (lower elevation) up to 60% across the higher \nelevations. It will take a bit for the PBL to moisten, especially \ngiven the light rainfall rates. This activity is thanks to an \nupper level low that is currently centered over central \nCalifornia. This low will continue to move east with precipitation\ndeveloping across eastern Nevada and western Utah this afternoon \nand evening. \n\nThis activity shifts into central and northern Utah on Tuesday \nwith morning showers transitioning to afternoon thunderstorms. \nThere will be plenty of shear (40+ kts) and steep lapse rates, \nalthough the CAPE will be marginal. This will favor quasi-linear \nstorms that will be capable of producing gusty winds for areas \nnear and east of the Wasatch and in Castle country. The storm \nmotion with these will be rather fast, so that will limit the \nthreat of any flash flooding to areas that are highly sensitive or\nareas where training thunderstorms occur.\n\nThis low pushes out of the area by Wednesday with a return to \nnear seasonal temperatures and dry/stable conditions. The one \nexception will be across eastern and southeastern Utah as moisture\nremains elevated.",
